# Slice 346 (the book, forms-declare-or-call): the tail of the form does not matter -- the result of a call. i is a
# known place of an int, so `i: f()` is refused (8) before anything of the tail is evaluated: f is not called.
fn: f () int (int: calls 0)
    calls := calls + 1
    return: 7
end: f
int: i 1
i: f()
int: after f\var\calls
